## User preferences

- When an application draft is “too generic,” use specific evidence for why this institution, why the work, and why now; avoid claims such as “frontier of machine intelligence” unless a concrete fact earns them. [Task 1]
- “use american english and remove-ai-slop and simplify the language” -> write in plain, direct, human American English; retain precise qualifiers rather than polished application clichés. [Task 1]
- Frame a disproved original claim constructively but honestly: “Testing showed that my original claim did not hold. I published the findings and used them to refine the research question.” [Task 1]

## Reusable knowledge

- Touchstone is evidence of empirical research discipline about checking work when only a sample can be checked; it is not itself a demonstrated general AI-safety or AI-control result. [Task 1]
- The sharp proposal question is “Which work should we check, and when, if agents know how we monitor them?” Proposed work compares random auditing with provider-history/time-since-joining selection at a fixed budget; measures undetected misconduct, attacker gains, and cost to honest providers; and keeps timing separate from selection. [Task 1]

